four years ago I survived one of the  worst natural disasters on record it was  January 12 2010 and I was in a meeting  with my colleagues in port-au-prince  Haiti when the walls of our building  started to shake scared and confused we  raced to the door only to realize that  the door was jammed shut we were trapped  when the shaking stopped we unjammed the  door and we raced down the stairs and as  I stepped over the threshold I was  welcomed by the outstretched arm of my  colleague and then I looked up and he  was covered in dust and white powder and  I started to look around in to my left  the cultural center vaporized the  primary school in the far corner  collapsed the building I had just come  out of was hanging by a thread and I  grasped the hand of my colleague and we  looked out onto the city that we called  home and it was barely recognizable the  next day I was completely overwhelmed by  what I saw collapsed homes and hospitals  family separated a country rocked to its  core I had this feeling of knots in my  stomach I wanted to do something but the  problem seems so much bigger than me and  I didn't know where to start I felt  hopeless  in that same moment on the other side of  town my colleague Paul was emerging from  the rubble of his collapsed four-story  building and instead of succumbing to  the feeling of helplessness that I felt  he was compelled to take action and  that's what I want to talk to you about  today I learned a profound lesson from  Paul that day it's about what I can do  and what you can do in the face of  overwhelming challenges Paul was a 24  year old I worked with me at the  microfinance bank I worked at in Haiti  he joined our organization because he  was really passionate about our mission  to deliver financial and educational  resources to some of the most vulnerable  communities in Haiti he had been quite  successful and had been known for  developing a really strong relationship  with his clients Paul was in his  apartment building when the earthquake  hit that day and collapsed with him  inside for 24 hours he was trapped  beneath the rubble of his home until his  neighbors came to dig him out in that  moment Paul had a choice he could have  let the situation overwhelm and paralyze  him but instead injured and dehydrated  he took a stick and he started walking  and step by step he walked to the home  of his first client to see if they were  okay and then he walked to the home of a  second client and then the third and  then the fourth and over and over step  by step door by door house by house Paul  went to check on each and every one of  his client  to see if they were okay the steps Paul  took that day had a huge impact for  months stories rolled in from his  clients with thank you saying how Paul's  visit that day brought them the hope  they needed in a moment of uncertainty  and despair because of Paul they knew  they were not alone and forgotten and  that hope led them to take their first  steps from finding a tarp for their roof  to picking fruit from a nearby tree to  pool with their neighbors or finally  just having the confidence to tell their  children that everything was going to be  okay Paul had a ripple effect and when  the stories of him and others like him  who took small such that day started to  roll in things started to move at my  organization one of our senior  executives started emailing and calling  all of our 900 plus employees a small  group of colleagues showed up to our  central office building the next day and  started salvaging what they could and I  finally showered and left my house for  the first time in three days we went on  to accomplish more than I ever could  have imagined in the weeks and months  following the earthquake I have thought  a lot about what Paul did that day I  think about the fear and paralysis that  I felt and I think about the strength  and courage that he demonstrated and I  wonder what did he know that I didn't  how did he find the strength and courage  he needed to take action in that moment  so one day I asked him and he responded  chipa chipa madam step by step madam  he knew he needed to do something he  decided on a path and then he took it  step by step and one door led to two  which led to three and things started to  spread now this expression t pas ce pas  it hearing it from Paul was not the  first time i heard it actually growing  up with a haitian father I remember  numerous times when I was facing an  obstacle or struggle he would tell me ce  pas ce pas Natalie step by step and then  I think about my mother who's  french-canadian and when she was  struggling with learning English and her  mother would tell her Fitzie opted see  step by step and then my husband news  from Senegal when he was coaching his  friend who is going through a difficult  time he is an expression in his native  language donka donk or step by step  across continents and cultures  everywhere I've looked I found this  unifying theme of using the concept of  step by step to get through a difficult  time so what did Paul do Paul took an  age old notion a universal piece of  wisdom and he put it into practice in a  powerful way because Paul knew taking  things step by step is the only way to  get things done so why is this important  to us here today I've had conversations  with many of you in this room who want  to solve problems or change a situation  and sometimes we feel overwhelmed we're  not sure where to start or which path to  take we worried about the impact will  have and we get stuck so I ask you  when's the last time you felt  overwhelmed by a situation for me it was  this past January 12th on the four year  anniversary of the earthquake well I was  proud of the things we accomplished  through news reports in  conversations with friends I was made  aware that there's still a lot of work  left to do and I was wondering what can  I do from here and those knots and my  stomach started to come back and then I  remembered Paul and I took my first step  I sent an email and that email turned  into one then three then five and more  of our classmates signing up to join me  on a trip to Haiti this spring break and  this trip will be their first steps in  understanding and learning about the  opportunities and obstacles that Haiti  faces so for me the first steps was an  email for the aspiring entrepreneur  who's sitting on an idea take your first  steps make that phone call share your  idea for the aspiring runner who's  wanting to get ready for the next big  race take your first step and put on  your running shoes for the new parents  who are worried if they're gonna do a  good job take your first step and  believe that you will so I ask of you  the next time you are feeling  overwhelmed remember tipa tipa and let  this wisdom of step-by-step take you  through it because overcoming life's  challenges can be as easy as taking a  single step  you
 

